sec.p:
To illustrate this effect, we define two normalized measures of the number of free viral particles after mixing with bacteria under each metabolic condition:
(1)
RAs/Az= Free viral particles after mixing with bacteria in As/Az Free viral particles in bacteria-free buffer with As/Az \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \mathrm{R}_{\mathrm{As} / \mathrm{Az}}=\frac{\text { Free viral particles after mixing with bacteria in As/Az }}{\text { Free viral particles in bacteria-free buffer with As/Az }} \tag{1} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
(2)
RGlu= Free viral particles after mixing with bacteria in Glu Free viral particles in bacteria-free buffer with Glu \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \mathrm{R}_{\mathrm{Glu}}=\frac{\text { Free viral particles after mixing with bacteria in Glu }}{\text { Free viral particles in bacteria-free buffer with Glu }} \tag{2} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}

Here, RAs/Az represents the relative abundance of free viruses under energy-depleted conditions, and RGlu represents the corresponding value under energy-competent conditions. The ratio of these two quantities captures how the host’s metabolic state influences viral commitment to infection. We define:
(3)
Ratio=RAs/AzRGlu \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \text { Ratio }=\frac{\mathrm{R}_{\mathrm{As} / \mathrm{Az}}}{\mathrm{R}_{\mathrm{Glu}}} \tag{3} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}

Crucially, this approach also allows us to compare the effect of different metabolic conditions on adsorption across different phages, as this measure is now independent of the duration of time over which we measure phage adsorption. Defining η′ as the adsorption rate in arsenate and azide, we can express the relative change in adsorption as:
(6)
η′η=−ln⁡RP,As/Az/(Btphage)−ln⁡RP,Glu/(Btphage)⇔η′η=ln⁡RP,As/Azln⁡RP,Glu. \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \frac{\eta^{\prime}}{\eta}=\frac{-\ln \mathrm{R}_{\mathrm{P}, \mathrm{As} / \mathrm{Az}} /\left(\mathrm{Bt}_{\mathrm{phage}}\right)}{-\ln \mathrm{R}_{\mathrm{P}, \mathrm{Glu}} /\left(\mathrm{Bt}_{\mathrm{phage}}\right)} \Leftrightarrow \frac{\eta^{\prime}}{\eta}=\frac{\ln \mathrm{R}_{\mathrm{P}, \mathrm{As} / \mathrm{Az}}}{\ln \mathrm{R}_{\mathrm{P}, \mathrm{Glu}}} . \tag{6} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}

Assuming that the bound state reaches a quasi-steady-state (d[PB]dt≈0)\begin{document}$\left(\frac{d[P B]}{d t} \approx 0\right)$\end{document}, the rate of formation of the bound complex is balanced by the combined rates of dissociation and irreversible commitment:
(7)
k⋅P⋅B=(koff+kcom)⋅[PB]⇔[PB]=k⋅P⋅Bkoff+kcom \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} k \cdot P \cdot B=\left(k_{\mathrm{off}}+k_{\mathrm{com}}\right) \cdot[P B] \quad \Leftrightarrow \quad[P B]=\frac{k \cdot P \cdot B}{k_{\mathrm{off}}+k_{\mathrm{com}}} \tag{7} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}

The rate of successful infection is determined by the number of complexes that proceed to irreversible DNA injection:
(8)
kcom[PB]=k1+koff/kcom⋅P⋅B \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} k_{\mathrm{com}}[P B]=\frac{k}{1+k_{\mathrm{off}} / k_{\mathrm{com}}} \cdot P \cdot B \tag{8} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}

This represents the rate at which free phages are irreversibly removed from the system. Considering a small time interval Δt, the fraction of phages lost is approximately:
(9)
ΔPP≈k1+koffkcom⋅B⋅Δt \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \frac{\Delta P}{P} \approx \frac{k}{1+\frac{k_{\mathrm{off}}}{k_{\mathrm{com}}}} \cdot B \cdot \Delta t \tag{9} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}

Connecting this to our mass-action model kinetics of phage adsorption, we have:
(10)
dPdt=−η⋅B⋅P⇒ΔPP≈η⋅B⋅Δt(for smallΔt)⇔η⋅B⋅Δt=k1+koffkcom⋅B⋅Δt⇔η=k1+koffkcom<k \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{align*} \frac{d P}{d t} & =-\eta \cdot B \cdot P \\ \Rightarrow \quad \frac{\Delta P}{P} & \approx \eta \cdot B \cdot \Delta t \quad(\text { for small } \Delta t) \\ \Leftrightarrow \quad \eta \cdot B \cdot \Delta t & =\frac{k}{1+\frac{k_{\text {off }}}{k_{\text {com }}}} \cdot B \cdot \Delta t \\ \Leftrightarrow \quad \eta & =\frac{k}{1+\frac{k_{\text {off }}}{k_{\text {com }}}}<k \tag{10} \end{align*}$$\end{document}
